Original commit body from 397b387d079c4cf4ebe4843644e51c50168fb59d:

The Elixir analyzer emitted one symbol per def clause, all named M.f, so
every multi-clause or multi-arity function rode the ADR-0035 backstop
floor and same-name groups were left stable_id=null (phoenix/lib: 678 of
2,231 nodes).

Clauses of one module/name/arity now fold into ONE function or macro
symbol (the Erlang shape): span first clause start to last clause end,
first clause's signature, parameters merged with any clause's defaults
(so def f(a, b \\ 1) is one f/2 node reachable at arity 1), CC
aggregated as Erlang does. Clauses split by another definition still
fold. The name stays M.f (call resolution, behaviour/protocol dispatch,
framework callbacks, routes and the catalogue key on it); arity is in
meta["parameters"]. The stable_id is minted through
overload_arity.declared_arity_stable_id with qualified_name=M.f; defstruct
fields get the same formula. A call in any clause anchors on the folded
function via a clause-position index (_clause_positions). Arity-narrowed
lookup now uses the shared narrow_by_arity (_reachable_clauses removed).

Floor -> producer only: the 59 (phoenix) / 1,470 (plausible) previously
producer-computed elixir ids (callbacks, protocol heads, modules, routes)
are byte-identical. Baseline: elixir removed from floor_and_null and
sibling_deletion_moves; the gate's elixir fixture's earlier sibling is now
a function of another arity.

Measured: phoenix/lib 2,231 -> 1,837 nodes, floor_abstained 678 -> 0;
plausible 13,916 -> 12,637 nodes, floor_abstained 1,943 -> 4 (the 4 are
not elixir).

